    "result_text": "Build passes (exit 0), all tests green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## What the change required\n\nThe spec is a cap on a new owner's `membershipLevel`. But the new acceptance test (`AcceptanceTest.coreCapsLevelByHousehold`) creates **two owners in the same household** (same last name + postcode, no `sharesHousehold` opt-in) and expects **both creates to succeed**, then checks the second's level is capped. The pre-existing `RejectDuplicateOwnerHousehold` rule returned `409` for exactly that case, so this checkpoint supersedes that blanket rejection with the level cap.\n\n## Changes\n\n**The cap (the core rule):**\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 added a persisted `membershipLevelCap` field (`membership_level_cap` column) + getter/setter, following the existing `householdSize`/`namesakeCount` pattern.\n- Added the column to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb/h2/mysql/postgres), nullable so pre-existing/seed owners have no cap.\n- New step `rest/function/owner/AssignOwnerMembershipLevelCap.java` \u2014 wired into `owners.POST.yml` before `save`. It finds existing household members via the shared `OwnerHouseholds.members(...)` helper and stores the ceiling = `max(member level) + 1`, reading each member's current level through `OwnerMapper.membershipLevel` (reuse, no duplicated formula). No members \u2192 cap stays `null` \u2192 no cap.\n- `mapper/OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 `membershipLevel` now clamps the derived level to the stored cap (`Math.min`) when present. This keeps the level formula in one place and means GET reflects the cap. Added `membershipLevelCap` to the `ignore` mappings on the two `toOwner(...)` methods.\n\n**Superseded rule removed cleanly:**\n- Removed the `rejectDuplicateHousehold` step from `owners.POST.yml` and deleted the now-unreachable `RejectDuplicateOwnerHousehold`, `DuplicateOwnerException`, `DuplicateOwnerExceptionHandler`, and its escalation YAML.\n- Fixed the dangling javadoc `{@link}`s that pointed at the removed class (`OwnerHouseholds`, `NormalizeOwnerAddress`, `AssignOwnerPossibleDuplicate`). Kept `sharesHousehold` \u2014 it's still used by `AssignOwnerPossibleDuplicate` to distinguish a declared household member from an accidental soft-duplicate.\n\nResult in the test run: owner A \u2192 `membershipLevel=1`, owner B (same household) \u2192 `membershipLevel=2` (= 1 + 1), satisfying `lb <= la + 1`.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: I inferred from the test that this checkpoint intentionally **removes** the household-duplicate 409 rejection (the test never opts in with `sharesHousehold` yet expects success). If instead you wanted household duplicates to still be rejected except in some other path, let me know and I'll revisit \u2014 but as written, both the spec paragraph (\"their household members\" for a *new* owner) and the test require household joining to be allowed.",
